# Tor as the analysis network's transparent proxy. DRAFT-057 Phase 2.
#
# Reference copy. Install on Dom0 as /etc/tor/torrc (or an include under
# /etc/tor/torrc.d/) and `systemctl restart tor`. Nothing in this repository
# deploys it -- the development container is not Dom0.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR, AND WHAT IT COSTS
#
# `tor` is one of the ways out a clone can be given per job. Its whole appeal
# is that it needs no purchase, no provider relationship and no VPS: it is the
# mode that lets real samples be detonated before an exit has been rented.
#
# Its cost is real and is surfaced on every report rather than argued away:
#
#   * Tor carries TCP only. UDP command-and-control and direct DNS DO NOT
#     LEAVE. They are dropped by the analysis firewall, because sending them
#     to a proxy that cannot carry them would be worse -- the sample would
#     hang instead of failing.
#   * Tor's default exit policy rejects 25, 119, 135-139, 445, 563 and several
#     P2P ranges. A spam bot's SMTP and an SMB worm's 445 will not appear.
#   * Exit operators are volunteers who absorb abuse complaints personally.
#
# The first two are recorded as `caveats` on the exit so the report can say
# "SMTP was blocked upstream" rather than showing nothing and letting a reader
# conclude the sample was quiet. That distinction is the whole reason this mode
# is safe to offer.

# --- the transparent proxy ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Bound on 0.0.0.0, and this needs justifying because it looks careless.
#
# A REDIRECT rewrites a packet's destination to the address of the interface it
# ARRIVED on -- which is a different gateway for every analysis VLAN
# (172.16.1.1, 172.16.2.1, ...). Binding per VLAN would mean one TransPort line
# per clone slot, regenerated and tor restarted whenever the clone count
# changes, for no gain.
#
# The exposure that creates -- these ports also listening on the uplink -- is
# closed in `automation/network-setup.sh`, which DROPs both ports arriving on
# xenbr0 as the FIRST rules in DRAKVUF_INPUT. Without those two rules this host
# is an open proxy for anyone who can reach it. They are not optional.
#
# **These port numbers appear in three places** -- here, `sandboxd/config.py`,
# and network-setup.sh's DROP rules -- and all three must agree. Change one
# alone and every clone's TCP is redirected to a port nothing listens on, which
# reads as a sample that tried to talk and got nowhere.
TransPort 0.0.0.0:9040
DNSPort 0.0.0.0:9053

# Onion addresses, for samples that use them.
#
# Tor hands out a synthetic address from this range for a .onion name and maps
# it back on connect. 10.192.0.0/10 sits inside the 10.0.0.0/8 that
# DRAKVUF_ISOLATION drops as RFC1918 -- harmless, because nat PREROUTING
# rewrites the destination to the gateway before FORWARD is consulted, so the
# packet never reaches that rule. Worth knowing before someone "fixes" the
# overlap.
AutomapHostsOnResolve 1
VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4 10.192.0.0/10

# --- what this tor is not ----------------------------------------------------

# No SOCKS port. Nothing on this host should be reaching Tor through a proxy
# it configured for itself; the only intended client is a redirected clone.
# `0` disables it entirely rather than binding it somewhere harmless.
SOCKSPort 0

# No relaying. This host must never carry other people's traffic: it would mix
# our analysis egress with strangers' and make the exit address' reputation
# somebody else's to spend.
ORPort 0
ExitRelay 0

# Log to syslog at notice. Enough to see circuits built and exits chosen --
# which is what makes "the sample could not connect" distinguishable from
# "Tor could not build a circuit" -- without recording per-connection detail
# about what a sample contacted. That belongs in the pcap, which is the
# evidence, not in a daemon log nobody collects.
Log notice syslog

# --- exit country selection (NOT enabled) ------------------------------------
#
# Country pinning is deliberately left off, and the reason is a trap worth
# recording rather than a preference.
#
#   ExitNodes {es}
#   StrictNodes 1
#
# `StrictNodes 1` makes Tor FAIL rather than fall back when no exit in that
# country will carry a circuit. That is the correct behaviour -- silently
# leaving from elsewhere would make the report's country field a lie -- but it
# means a run can fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the sample, and
# nothing currently distinguishes those two on the report.
#
# Enable it only alongside a caveat that says so. Country selection proper
# belongs to `routed`, where the exit is a machine we rent and its location is
# a fact rather than a request.